1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:14,909
Early in 1221, a Mongol army surrounded Merv, one of the great cities of Central Asia. After it surrendered, its people were led out onto the plain, and most of them were killed.

2
00:00:16,009 --> 00:00:26,911
A chronicler of the time claimed seven hundred thousand died there. Modern historians think that number is impossible. But Merv never regained its old greatness.

3
00:00:28,011 --> 00:00:37,973
Estimates for all the Mongol conquests run into the tens of millions. Yet the man who began them is honoured today as the founder of a nation.

4
00:00:39,073 --> 00:00:50,060
Genghis Khan is remembered as one of history's most destructive conquerors, and in Mongolia as its greatest hero. Both memories rest on real events.

5
00:00:52,260 --> 00:01:03,993
He was born around 1162, near the Onon River in what is now Mongolia, and named Temüjin. His father, Yesügei, was a minor chief.

6
00:01:05,093 --> 00:01:18,405
The steppe was a world of feuding clans who herded horses and sheep and lived in felt tents. Most of what we know of his youth comes from one source, The Secret History of the Mongols, written after his death.

7
00:01:19,505 --> 00:01:31,580
When Temüjin was about nine, his father was poisoned by enemies from the Tatar tribe. His clan abandoned the widow and her children, who survived by gathering roots and fishing.

8
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:40,125
As a teenager he killed his half-brother, Begter, in a quarrel over food. His mother condemned him furiously.

9
00:01:41,225 --> 00:01:51,337
Later he was captured by a rival clan and held with a heavy wooden collar locked around his neck. He escaped at night, hidden by a sympathetic family.

10
00:01:53,537 --> 00:01:59,489
Temüjin grew into a shrewd and magnetic young leader, gathering followers one by one.

11
00:02:00,589 --> 00:02:10,979
He married Börte, the girl he had been promised to as a boy. When raiders from the Merkit tribe kidnapped her, he won her back with the help of two powerful allies.

12
00:02:12,079 --> 00:02:22,468
One was Toghrul, khan of the Kereit people. The other was Jamukha, his childhood friend and sworn blood brother. Both would later become his enemies.

13
00:02:23,568 --> 00:02:33,787
Over the next twenty years he fought his way to the top. He promoted men for loyalty and talent rather than noble birth, and shared plunder with his followers.

14
00:02:34,887 --> 00:02:40,391
He crushed the Tatars, who had killed his father, and had most of their men put to death.

15
00:02:41,491 --> 00:02:49,683
He defeated Toghrul, then the Naimans, and finally Jamukha, who was betrayed by his own followers and executed.

16
00:02:50,783 --> 00:03:04,009
In 1206, a great assembly of the tribes proclaimed him Genghis Khan. The exact meaning of the title is debated. It may mean fierce ruler, or universal ruler.

17
00:03:06,209 --> 00:03:19,180
He reorganised his people into units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand, mixing up the old tribes so that loyalty ran to him. He adopted a writing system and a code of law.

18
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:30,648
Every soldier was a mounted archer, with several horses each. Mongol armies moved fast, scouted far ahead, and were masters of the feigned retreat.

19
00:03:31,748 --> 00:03:41,903
He also began a system of relay stations, where messengers could change horses, carrying orders across vast distances at remarkable speed.

20
00:03:43,003 --> 00:03:57,019
His method was simple and brutal. Cities that surrendered were usually spared, though heavily taxed. Cities that resisted were often destroyed, and their people killed or enslaved, as a warning to the next.

21
00:03:59,219 --> 00:04:11,251
In 1209 he forced the Western Xia kingdom to submit. In 1211 he invaded the Jin dynasty, which ruled northern China.

22
00:04:12,351 --> 00:04:21,609
In 1215 the Jin capital, Zhongdu, today's Beijing, fell after a long siege, and was sacked and burned.

23
00:04:22,709 --> 00:04:32,651
He was also a fast learner. Captured Chinese engineers taught the Mongols siege warfare, and skilled craftsmen were often spared and put to work.

24
00:04:34,851 --> 00:04:49,592
To the west lay the Khwarazmian Empire, stretching across Central Asia and Iran. In 1218, a trade caravan sent by Genghis Khan was massacred at the border city of Otrar.

25
00:04:50,692 --> 00:04:59,929
He sent envoys to demand justice. The Shah, Muhammad the Second, had one of them executed and sent the others back humiliated.

26
00:05:01,029 --> 00:05:13,787
In 1219 Genghis Khan invaded with a huge army. Over the next three years, Bukhara, Samarkand, Urgench, Merv and Nishapur fell one after another.

27
00:05:14,887 --> 00:05:23,420
At Bukhara, according to the Persian historian Juvayni, he told the city's leading men that he was God's punishment for their sins.

28
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:40,520
Chroniclers recorded death tolls of hundreds of thousands, even more than a million, for single cities. Ibn al-Athir and Juvayni gave these figures for Merv. Modern historians consider them impossible, but agree the slaughter was immense.

29
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:50,665
Irrigation systems were wrecked and farmland turned to waste. Some historians argue parts of the region took centuries to recover.

30
00:05:51,765 --> 00:05:57,461
The Shah fled west and died on an island in the Caspian Sea, his empire gone.

31
00:05:58,561 --> 00:06:11,297
Two of Genghis Khan's generals rode on around the Caspian, through the Caucasus, and defeated an army of Rus' princes in 1223, before turning home.

32
00:06:13,497 --> 00:06:32,463
In 1226 he turned on the Western Xia, who had refused to send troops for his western war. He died during the campaign, in August 1227. The cause is unknown. One account says he never recovered from a fall from his horse.

33
00:06:33,563 --> 00:06:39,600
The Western Xia kingdom was destroyed soon afterwards, and many of its people were killed.

34
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:46,225
He was buried in secret, somewhere in Mongolia. His grave has never been found.

35
00:06:48,425 --> 00:07:06,836
His sons and grandsons carried the conquests further: across Russia, into Hungary and Poland, to Baghdad in 1258, and over all of China by 1279. It became the largest contiguous land empire in history.

36
00:07:07,936 --> 00:07:20,565
In northern China, census figures fell by tens of millions within a generation. Historians caution that part of the drop reflects flight, chaos and people who simply went uncounted.

37
00:07:21,665 --> 00:07:36,663
Once the conquests ended, the empire linked China, Persia and Europe. Merchants and envoys, like the Venetian Marco Polo decades later, crossed it in relative safety, and goods and ideas moved between continents.

38
00:07:37,763 --> 00:07:47,896
The same routes may also have helped carry the Black Death west in the 1340s, though historians still debate exactly how.

39
00:07:50,096 --> 00:08:05,349
In 2003, a genetic study found that about one in two hundred men alive today carry a Y-chromosome lineage that the authors suggested came from Genghis Khan's family. Not all scientists agree.

40
00:08:06,449 --> 00:08:23,836
In Mongolia, his memory was played down under Soviet influence. Since the democratic revolution of 1990, he has returned as the father of the nation, on banknotes, on the capital's airport, and as a giant steel statue on the steppe.

41
00:08:24,936 --> 00:08:35,517
To Mongolians, he made a scattered people into a nation and a world power. In Iran, Central Asia and China, he is remembered for the cities that vanished.

42
00:08:36,617 --> 00:08:47,028
Both memories are true. His empire connected the world on a scale never seen before, and it was built on a scale of killing that is almost as hard to imagine.
